    <div class="doc-panel">
      <h2>State change first, event alongside it</h2>
      <p>
        Session state and its creation event are written in one database
        transaction. The event stream carries monotonically increasing IDs so a
        client can reconnect from a cursor and recover what it missed.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="doc-panel">
      <h2>Permission before effect</h2>
      <p>
        The model can propose a bounded replacement for one text file. Veasel
        saves its diff without touching the workspace. In the normal TUI flow,
        the user opens that diff before a one-time approval can apply it;
        application verifies the reviewed file snapshot and uses an atomic
        replacement. Shell commands require a separate review of the exact
        command, workspace directory, and timeout before a one-time approval.
        Commands inherit the server user's environment. When a platform sandbox
        backend is available, approved shell wraps with Bubblewrap (Linux),
        Seatbelt (macOS), or AppContainer+Job (Windows); otherwise it falls
        through unsandboxed. The loopback API has no application token, so
        same-user processes can call approval routes. Interrupted file writes
        and shell commands are not replayed automatically.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="doc-panel">
      <h2>What stays out of marketing math</h2>
      <p>
        Feature presence is not a task-resolution score. Cross-agent latency,
        cost-per-solved-task, and Harbor Index / Terminal-Bench / SWE-bench
        leaderboards are not claimed here. See{" "}
        <a href={`${basePath}/benchmarks/`}>Benchmarks</a> for measured
        process-startup artifacts only.
      </p>
